 "prompt": "The repository at /testbed has a bug, described below. Fix it by changing the source code; do not modify or\nadd tests. When you are done, the project's existing test suite should pass.\n\n<issue>\nElementProxy equality comparison broken after recent changes\n\n#### Description\n\nThe `__ne__` method in ElementProxy is returning incorrect results, causing equality comparisons to behave unexpectedly. When comparing two ElementProxy instances, the inequality operator always returns `False` regardless of whether the objects are actually equal or not.\n\n#### Steps/Code to Reproduce\n\n```python\nfrom pptx.shared import ElementProxy\nfrom lxml import etree\n\n# Create two different elements\nelem1 = etree.Element('test1')\nelem2 = etree.Element('test2')\n\nproxy1 = ElementProxy(elem1)\nproxy2 = ElementProxy(elem2)\n\n# These should be different, but __ne__ returns False\nprint(f\"proxy1 != proxy2: {proxy1 != proxy2}\")  # Expected: True, Actual: False\n\n# Create two proxies for the same element\nproxy3 = ElementProxy(elem1)\nprint(f\"proxy1 != proxy3: {proxy1 != proxy3}\")  # Expected: False, Actual: False\n```\n\nThe `__ne__` method seems to have logic issues where it's not properly checking the relationship between the underlying elements. This affects any code that relies on ElementProxy inequality comparisons.\n</issue>\n",
